# Regression test: an external `cmd` whose process dies from an ordinary OS signal
# (not through crush's own `terminate`) must not abort the rest of the script.
#
# Root cause: `spawn_child_wait`'s waiter thread (src/builtins/control/cmd.rs) used to
# route *any* signal-caused child death through the full `GlobalState::terminate`, which
# broadcasts a real `StreamControlMessage::Terminate` to every controller registered for
# the job -- including `wait_for_child_inner`'s own control-channel listener, which
# treats that message as an explicit termination request and returns an error. That
# made `cmd` itself fail (aborting the enclosing script) even for a signal the user
# never asked crush to send -- here, the child process terminates *itself* with
# `kill -TERM $$`, something a perfectly ordinary external command might do for its own
# reasons, with nothing to do with crush's job control at all.
#
# Fixed by routing a signal-caused death through `GlobalState::mark_terminated_by_signal`
# instead: bookkeeping only (status + the one-time `jobs` snapshot, see
# `tests/terminated_job_is_reaped.crush`), with no controller broadcast.
#
# No echoed marker needed: a top-level job's error already aborts the rest of the
# script with a nonzero exit (see `execute.rs`'s `source()`), which the test runner
# already checks against the implicit expected `0` -- if this regressed, the process
# would exit nonzero on its own, with nothing further to print.
cmd sh -c "kill -TERM $$"
